    {
      "id": 1,
      "claim": "An item can be safely reused only if it can be fully decontaminated and is not a single‑use consumable.",
      "evidence": "Laboratory safety guidelines (e.g., OSHA, CDC) require that any material exposed to unknown or hazardous substances be either thoroughly decontaminated or discarded; single‑use items are defined as those not intended for cleaning and reuse.",
      "weight": 0.18,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "claim": "Glassware used to heat liquids is non‑porous and can be cleaned, rinsed, and sterilized for reuse.",
      "evidence": "Standard lab practice (e.g., USP <797>, institutional SOPs) mandates washing glassware with detergent, thorough rinsing, and autoclaving or oven‑drying before it is reused in another investigation.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "Filter paper cannot be safely reused because its porous matrix retains particulates and chemicals, and cleaning cannot guarantee removal of contaminants.",
      "evidence": "SOPs for filtration (e.g., EPA Method 162, laboratory protocols) state that filter paper is a disposable consumable; attempts to wash or autoclave filter paper do not restore its original pore structure and leave residual contaminants, making cross‑contamination likely.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "Paper towels are single‑use absorbents that become saturated with liquids and cannot be effectively decontaminated.",
      "evidence": "Manufacturers label paper towels as disposable; laboratory waste management policies require disposal after use because the fibers trap liquids and any cleaning would leave residues.",
      "weight": 0.12,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "Powdered chemicals recovered from a spill are considered contaminated and their purity cannot be assured, so they must be discarded.",
      "evidence": "Chemical hygiene plans (e.g., ACS guidelines) require that any reagent involved in a spill be treated as waste, because it may have mixed with unknown substances and its concentration is unknown.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "claim": "Therefore, the only item among the options that can be safely reused after an investigation is the glassware (Option A).",
      "evidence": "Combining steps 2‑5 with the reuse criterion from step 1 shows that only glassware meets the requirement; all other items fail due to being single‑use or contaminated.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [2, 3, 4, 5]
    }
  ],
  "answer": "A",
  "confidence": 0.96,
